 {:seon.task/name "contract-gaps-have-one-rule"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen 2026-10-21 by session AGENTS: which contract gaps an attempt answers for is decided in three places. The turn loop takes stage/uncontracted now minus the gaps when the attempt began (seon.turn/begin!, observe, turn.clj); root's judge takes the branch's program entries that have no contract and differ from main's (judge-tx, reconcile.clj:588-600); and before e37b318 the server's context read took every gap (it showed 400 seeded app.m*/f* gaps from seon.db's seed program as errors). Two rules can disagree: a gap main gained after the branch point, or a var the agent re-evaluated unchanged. Correct: one function, the judge's (gaps on the branch's program entries that differ from main's base), called by the turn loop, the context and the judge alike. Deletes :gaps-before."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/the-loop-the-context-and-the-judge-name-the-same-gaps}}
 {:seon.task/name "render-reads-the-reconcilers-index"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-10-21 by session RENDER on its own root (bin/root, 92 tasks, 13 attempts, 17 agents, 1,820 datoms; 20-run means through the server): seon.render.proc/main-info costs 113 ms per main commit, rebuilding from main's db what the reconciler already keeps incrementally: seon.reconcile/index 58 ms, seon.task/tasks 26 ms, seon.task/attempts 8 ms, seon.problems/report 5 ms, the datom count 0.7 ms (render/proc.clj main-info). A quiet render pass is 0.87 ms, so the commit path dominates. Build once: the reconciler emits each task row (state, blocked-by, proofs green/red/allowed, attempts, tokens), each attempt and the recorded problems as it changes them, through THREADS' event principle, and render reads them; render's task-rows, its index rebuild and its per-commit seon.task/tasks and attempts go. Estimate: per main commit, work in proportion to the tasks and attempts that commit touched, not the whole index."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-test/render-rebuilds-no-index-per-commit
                      seon.render-test/render-main-commit-cost}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-design-step-designs"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-10-21 on the standing root: task `seon.db/a-store-opens-where-it-lies` capped at 8 turns (98k tokens, proof red), and the refinement `seon.template/attach-tx` attached began with `a-store-opens-where-it-lies-r1-design`, which ended `:done :green` with 0 turns and 0 tokens. `seon.reconcile/done-errors` lets a `:design` attempt's proofs stay red, so at its start nothing refuses it and the green-at-start rule ends it with no model call; the implement step then got the failed task's plan unchanged. A design step's work is the plan and interface the next step reads (`seon.template/instructions` :design: decide how the change fits main, write each function's defn with its contract, add a cost proof for a growing input, improve the plan with `my.task/update!`). Correct: a `:design` attempt is never done at its start, and is done only when this attempt improved the task's plan (the accumulated plan differs from the one it started with, read from the attempt's facts, not a flag the agent sets); otherwise it is refused `:no-design`, shown in the context with the instruction it lacks. The other kinds' rule is unchanged. Extends `seon.reconcile/done-errors` and its input (`:seon.reconcile/done-input`), nothing new beside them."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/a-design-step-is-not-done-at-its-start
                      seon.reconcile-test/a-design-step-is-done-when-it-improved-the-plan}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-refused-spawn-is-recorded"
  :seon.task/plan "Audit FENCES #20. When root refuses a spawn (`seon.root` answers its `:spawn` op with `:seon.root/refused`: the branch is held by a live agent, e.g. an outside attach, or `:seon.config.root/max-agents` agents are live), the reconciler's `:spawned` step in `seon.reconcile/advance` gets an answer with no `:id` and only ends the flight: no attempt, no fact, no problem. The task stays ready and is retried only when another commit wakes the reconciler, and `seon.reconcile/stuck` lists only stuck facts and unreleased tasks, so at quiescence a task that cannot start is invisible. Correct: the error is written on main as the attempt, status `:refused` with root's message as `:seon.attempt/reason` and the error map (as a dead attempt is), which needs `:refused` in `:seon.attempt/status` (change a schema at your REPL with `my.schema/register!`: the merge writes it back to its EDN file); `seon.reconcile/stuck` reports a task whose latest attempt is refused as a root cause (`{:task :cause the error message :attempt :blocks}`); the next pass starts it again as the next attempt. Replaces the silent `(done st)` in the `:spawned` step. Proofs written red by root 2026-10-21: the first fails because nothing is written, the second because the status schema refuses `:refused`. Done (session fences-spend, 2026-10-22, moved here from the guards file): the `:spawned` step writes the error through `seon.task/attempt-start-tx` with no agent (`:seon.attempt/agent` is optional: a refused attempt has none), status `:refused`, root's message and the error map; `seon.reconcile/stuck` names a task whose latest attempt (by its number) is refused. Found on the way: the old `(done st)` also asked again at once (the flight's end runs `seon.reconcile/pass`, the task still ready), a hot loop of refused spawns while the branch stayed held; written as attempts it would grow main without bound. So a refused task waits in the proc's `:refused` until main moves for another reason: `seon.reconcile/reconciler-proc`'s `:main` clears it on any work commit that is not only refused attempts (if nothing has changed, asking again is refused again). `seon.e2e/agents-reused` counts attempts by agent and would count the refused ones' absent agent: filed with root."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/a-refused-spawn-writes-a-refused-attempt
                      seon.reconcile-test/quiescence-names-a-task-that-cannot-start
                      seon.reconcile-test/a-refused-task-asks-again-when-main-moves}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-refused-flight-write-is-not-ignored"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen by session RUNNER (jolt/runner e0efb69, 2026-10-21): the run's store hit MDB_MAP_FULL; a check step's child exited, the reconciler's :ran step wrote the attempt's end, the writer refused it (seon.writer/writer-one answers :seon.writer/refused), and seon.reconcile/advance's :done step dropped the answer (the `:done (done st)` case, still so on main): the attempt stayed :running, so the reconciler never reached quiet and nothing said why. Every flight's write takes that path (:spawned, :started, :ran, :octopus-merged, then :done), an agent's attempt included; the refused spawn (:refused attempts) covers spawns only. Correct: an answer that is an error map is read: the flight records it where it can (the attempt's :seon.attempt/error by a smaller write, or the reconciler's state and ping-map-fn as :refused with the attempt and message when main refuses every write), and quiescence counts the flight as ended with that error, so the run ends and names the error. The branch's fix (b880791, WIP) was built on :check tasks; its proof is re-targeted to an agent's attempt."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/a-refused-end-write-is-reported-at-quiet}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-train-is-proven-by-one-combined-run"
  :seon.task/plan "From jolt/r5-port (302ce17, parked WIP; notes/option3-R5-trains.md): an octopus merge's members are proven together by one combined test run of the tests their changes reach, and on red the octopus merge is bisected (halves re-run) to name the member that broke it; the green members merge. Main has octopus merges (the :octopus-merge op) and reach selection, but seon.fences/merge-trains-off-as-decided keeps octopus merges off until this exists, and no task built it. Re-derive on main's reconciler and seon.agent/run-tests-forked; the branch is reference only (reconcile.clj +527/-310 on an old base). Replaces the off switch."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/a-red-octopus-merge-is-bisected-to-its-member}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-outside-attempts-agent-death-ends-it"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen 2026-10-21 by session DOOR (jolt/door 436f489), still true on main e117b9b: an outside attempt (`seon.server/attach!`, model \"outside\") is written by the server, and a reconciler-started outside attempt's flight ends at `:started` (`seon.reconcile/advance`), so nothing observes its agent's death: the attempt stays `:running` with its agent `:dead`, counts against `:max-running`, and a waiter (a check step, a test) hangs. A model attempt's death is observed only as the answer to its flight's pending eval (`seon.reconcile/dead-tx` via `:dead-read`). Root records the death on main as a problem (`seon.root/record-death`), but no fact names the attempt. Correct: an agent's death is a fact the reconciler observes (root writes it on main with the agent's id when it sees the agent's end), so every `:running` attempt whose agent died ends `:dead` through `dead-tx`, outside or not. Replaces the flight-only death path. Touches root's death recording (session reds owns root's proc answers) and the reconciler."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.server-test/an-outside-attempt-ends-dead-when-its-agent-dies}}]
